//// Front 12 step 4.4 — "no external target for the active backend". A
//// host-backed `declare fn` bound to one host is a program for that host: on
//// erlang (and beam, which consumes the Erlang vocabulary) it runs and prints
//// `hi`; on commonJS the compiler **refuses** it, naming the declaration and the
//// backend — `external_erlang_only.commonJS.expect` carries that diagnostic, the
//// shape of a `reject/` cell on one target.
////
//// `external_erlang_only.targets` keeps wasm out of the cell. Measured at
//// `85f883bd` (2026-09-20): wasm does **not** refuse it — `wat.zig`
//// (`lowerPlainCall`) lowers a host-backed `declare fn` with no wasm host to
//// `unreachable` on purpose, "so the module still loads", and the program traps
//// at run time (exit 134, stdout empty) where the other three backends answer at
//// compile time. That is a divergence with no owner row in
//// `specs/1.0.10-beta/00-compiler-carry-over/README.md`, so it is reported here
//// and in AGENTS.md rather than listed against an invented one.

#[@External.Erlang("erlang", "list_to_binary")]
declare fn listToBinary(codes: i32[]) -> string;

pub fn main() {
    @print(listToBinary([104, 105]));
}
